  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nLow level upslope flow will spark off storms over eastern \nColorado later this afternoon and evening. The activity will \nslowly spread east tonight as low level jet increases the \nmoisture transport leading/feeding the complex of storms. \nLimited amount of instability/shear will keep storms below \nsevere levels for central/eastern Kansas. There is some \npotential for heavy rainfall with high precipitable water \nvalues. The activity looks to linger through the morning hours \non Thursday, mainly for locations along and east of the Kansas \nTurnpike. Meanwhile, current satellite water vapor imagery shows\nembedded upper level wave with-in deep tropical plume over Utah\nmoving northward. Models show this system being picked up by \nthe west/northwest flow regime aloft and steered eastward across\nNebraska/Kansas for Thursday night into Friday. This will bring\nmore showers/storms to the region along with an uptick for \ncoverage. Severe weather chances will remain low given limited \nwind shear/instability combination, some areas could see locally\nheavy rainfall.\n\nHeading into the weekend models show upper level ridge shifting \neastward into the central plains which will cause the heat dome to \nexpand over Kansas. This pattern will persist into next week with \nhotter temperatures and dry weather conditions likely to persist. \nHeat indices look to climb into the dangerous range with heat \nadvisories needed, and the longer duration of expected heat could \npotentially lead to a Extreme Heat Warning by Tuesday or Wednesday \nif trends continue.",
